-- chem/atom.cub — the atom exactly: hydrogen's shells, their counts and their energies, with no real
-- number. Pauli (1926) solved the Coulomb problem algebraically, through the conserved Runge–Lenz vector,
-- whose so(4) symmetry fixes the bound energies as −1/(2n²) Hartree and each level's states as the
-- orbitals l = 0 … n−1 with 2l+1 projections each. So the shell structure of the periodic table and the
-- spectrum of hydrogen are integers and rationals: the counts are proved here for every n, and the
-- energies are exact rationals in the Hartree, the one measured scale (alpha-inv, mp-over-me in
-- chem/fold.cub carry the constants that convert it; the finite proton mass multiplies every level by
-- the reduced-mass ratio 1/(1 + me/mp), itself a ratio of measured values).
import "spin.hypercubical"

